        "narration": "Three knocks sound beneath the table. A wire catches the candlelight. The spirit board spells TONIC, but the mechanism, not a ghost, moved it. Ambrose hired Vesper to stage a performance; the script has not yet been examined.\n\nA servant brings down Eleanor's old tonic bottle, kept at Ambrose's request for the constable. Its label bears S.A. in Ashgrove's handwriting. In the conservatory, monkshood stems have been freshly cut; soil leads toward the physician's chair. Monkshood is also called wolfsbane. Its poison can produce the symptoms seen on Ambrose.\n\nAccounts, letters and household complaints now expose the disputes surrounding tonight: Constance's gambling debt, Crane's stolen silver, and Vesper's threatened exposure as a fraud. The bottle links the present investigation to Eleanor's death a year ago. It does not yet show how tonight's poison reached the victim.\n\n{pell} lays out the garden findings. {ashgrove} reads the tonic label aloud. {vesper} places the discovered wire beside the performance account.\nWhich dispute connects to the medicine? In this 7-player edition, the comparison passes through the whole table:\n{ashgrove} leads the comparison concerning {constance}.\n{crane} leads the comparison concerning {vesper}.\n{constance} leads the comparison concerning {grey}.\n{vesper} leads the comparison concerning {marsh}.\n{grey} leads the comparison concerning {pell}.\n{marsh} leads the comparison concerning {ashgrove}.\n{pell} leads the comparison concerning {crane}.\nEvery guest reads the findings below on their phone; nobody acts out a discovery.",
